                <h2>Lead with the Conclusion: Compliance Audits Are Your Safety Net, Not an Option</h2>
<p>Many teams treat compliance audits as a reactive measure—something to do after problems arise. In reality, once a compliance issue occurs, you're looking at account suspensions at best, permanent bans at worst. You don't just lose that day's budget; you lose accumulated ad data and learning history. <strong>Compliance audits should be a standard pre-launch procedure, not damage control after the fact.</strong> This SOP is what I've developed through five years of managing overseas ad accounts, learning from mistakes the hard way.</p>
<h2>Account-Level Audit: Where 80% of Bans Originate</h2>
<h3>Credentials and Verification</h3>
<p>Before activating any overseas ad account, confirm three things:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Business credentials match your推广 category</strong>: Promoting financial products? Make sure your account has the required regulatory certifications. Using fake or exaggerated credentials is a leading cause of account bans.</li>
<li><strong>Business license matches account registration</strong>: Inconsistencies make appeals difficult later.</li>
<li><strong>Apply for whitelist for restricted industries</strong>: Medical, gambling, crypto—these categories require pre-approval from the platform.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Payment Information</h3>
<ul>
<li>Ensure credit cards or payment accounts have sufficient balance to avoid triggering risk controls from failed charges.</li>
<li>Avoid linking the same credit card to multiple ad accounts—this can trigger platform association risk flags.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Account Structure</h3>
<ul>
<li>Clearly map each ad account to its corresponding Business Manager (BM) assets; avoid asset mixing.</li>
<li>Apply the principle of minimum necessary permissions—only grant access to those who truly need it.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Ad-Level Audit: Creative and Copy Are the Problem Areas</h2>
<h3>Creative Compliance</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Image assets</strong>: Avoid unauthorized brand logos, celebrity photos, or copyrighted film screenshots. One utility app had their entire account suspended for three days after using a popular movie screenshot in their ad creatives.</li>
<li><strong>Video assets</strong>: Check background music for copyright restrictions; watch for absolute claims in subtitles (e.g., "100% effective," "guaranteed returns").</li>
<li><strong>Copy</strong>: Exaggerated claims, fake urgency tactics, and misleading content about sensitive groups (children, elderly) are common violations.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Policy Adaptation</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>GDPR compliance</strong>: For EU campaigns, landing pages must have a Cookie consent pop-up, and data collection must stop if users opt out.</li>
<li><strong>Regional审核 standards vary</strong>: Southeast Asia is relatively lenient; North America and Europe are stricter. The same creative can receive completely different review outcomes in different regions.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Case in point</strong>: An e-commerce team promoting in the EU had their Meta ad account suspended because their landing page Cookie pop-up was non-compliant (pre-checked consent boxes). Post-mortem revealed the team had only reviewed ad creatives, ignoring the landing page's technical compliance requirements.</p>
</blockquote>
<h2>The Audit SOP: 3 Checkpoints, 1 Mechanism</h2>
<h3>Checkpoint 1: Pre-Launch Self-Audit</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Item</th>
<th>Standard</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Credentials</td>
<td>Valid and match推广 category</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Creative copyright</td>
<td>No infringing content</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Copy claims</td>
<td>No absolute promises, no misleading info</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Landing page compliance</td>
<td>Privacy policy complete, Cookie pop-up compliant</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>Checkpoint 2: Post-Launch Regular Audits (Weekly recommended)</h3>
<ul>
<li>Account health: Any unresolved policy violation notices?</li>
<li>Ad data anomalies: Sudden CTR drops or abnormal conversion rate fluctuations.</li>
<li>Spend anomalies: Unusual spending during off-peak hours could indicate click fraud or system errors.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Checkpoint 3: Emergency Response Protocol</h3>
<p>When you receive an ad rejection or account suspension notice:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Within 24 hours</strong>: Identify the violation reason—creative issue or policy misunderstanding?</li>
<li><strong>Within 48 hours</strong>: Modify creatives or submit an appeal to prevent escalation.</li>
<li><strong>Policy update sync</strong>: Platform policies change regularly; review Meta and Google's official policy announcements monthly and communicate changes to your team promptly.</li>
</ol>
<h2>Final Thoughts</h2>
<p>Compliance audits don't require complex systems—they require consistent habits. Start with self-audits before every ad launch, then gradually build regular audit routines and emergency response mechanisms. <strong>Controlling risk before ads go live is the most cost-effective approach to operations.</strong></p>
